WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.920 line:91%
This is a United States
one-dollar bill.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.740 position:70% line:85%
It's just over a tenth of a millimetre thick.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.740 position:59% line:85%
So, what if you had a million of them?

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.860 line:91%
One million US dollars
stacked up into the sky?

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.880 line:85%
How tall would that stack be?

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.480 line:91%
Well, I mean, first of all,
it would fall over.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.320 line:91%
So let's say instead that
you put that stack on its side.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.160 line:91%
One after the other
after the other, lined up.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.460 position:59% line:85%
Not edge to edge, but just stacked up.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.500 line:85%
How long would that stack be?

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.200 position:67% line:91%
Well, it'd be about the length of
a US football field or a UK football pitch.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.580 line:91%
So, that's the distance
that I'm gonna walk, right now.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.480 line:91%
And as we go, have a think about
just how much money that is.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.140 position:56% line:91%
We passed $10,000 within
my first couple of steps back there,

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.340 position:72% line:91%
and that's more money than
the average annual wage in a lot of the world.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.400 position:58% line:91%
Now, there's a money clock on-screen,
tied to the GPS in this camera,

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.240 line:91%
so the numbers should be
about right as we go.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.360 position:66% line:85%
A lot of folks have created visualisations

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.140 line:91%
of the difference between
a million and a billion.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.800 position:61% line:91%
Humphrey Yang on TikTok did a great one
with grains of rice,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.460 position:72% line:91%
and Wren at Corridor Digital did
this spectacular visual effects demonstration.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.700 position:75% line:91%
but where I think those visualisations fall down
is that they rely on volume.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.440 position:58% line:91%
They rely on three-dimensional stuff,
and that's just not something

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.840 line:91%
that the human brain is
particularly good at.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.920 position:72% line:91%
It's just not intuitive that if you multiply
the height, width, and depth of a cube by ten,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.040 position:63% line:85%
then the volume increases by a thousand.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.000 line:91%
Yes, mathematically obvious
if you know the formula,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.620 position:63% line:91%
but intuitively, it's just not that easy
to actually understand.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:30.200 position:73% line:91%
If you really want to understand the difference
between a million and a billion,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.400 line:91%
then you need something
one-dimensional.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.160 line:85%
You need distance.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:40.760 line:91%
So, I am gonna drive the length of
one billion dollars.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.580 line:91%
It doesn't feel like
it should take that long, right?

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:48.060 position:63% line:91%
After all, I walked to a million dollars
in a minute or so.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.680 line:91%
How long could it take
to get to a billion

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.400 line:91%
when you've got a car
and some fast roads?

